1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,095
A tailor measures cloth.

2
00:00:02,095 --> 00:00:03,944
A builder measures a floor.

3
00:00:03,944 --> 00:00:05,395
A painter, a wall.

4
00:00:05,745 --> 00:00:07,386
All three combine lengths.

5
00:00:07,386 --> 00:00:09,698
And every length hides a small error.

6
00:00:10,148 --> 00:00:12,299
Aarav measures his study table.

7
00:00:12,299 --> 00:00:15,392
One hundred and twenty
centimetres, by sixty.

8
00:00:15,742 --> 00:00:19,122
His ruler can be off by one
millimetre on each side.

9
00:00:19,472 --> 00:00:21,455
So how far off is the area?

10
00:00:21,455 --> 00:00:22,957
One square millimetre?

11
00:00:22,957 --> 00:00:24,322
It is much bigger.

12
00:00:24,872 --> 00:00:26,766
So what is error propagation?

13
00:00:27,116 --> 00:00:31,676
When we calculate with measured values,
their errors travel into the result.

14
00:00:32,026 --> 00:00:34,100
Propagation simply means spreading.

15
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,203
We always take the worst case, so
the true value surely lies inside our

16
00:00:39,203 --> 00:00:40,103
range.

17
00:00:40,358 --> 00:00:41,514
First, adding.

18
00:00:41,514 --> 00:00:43,698
Two rods on our measuring bench.

19
00:00:44,048 --> 00:00:48,086
Rod A is twenty centimetres,
plus or minus zero point one.

20
00:00:48,436 --> 00:00:51,964
Rod B is fifteen, plus
or minus zero point one.

21
00:00:52,314 --> 00:00:55,430
End to end, the total is
thirty five centimetres.

22
00:00:55,780 --> 00:00:58,180
Let both rods be at their longest.

23
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:00,580
The total grows by zero point two.

24
00:01:00,930 --> 00:01:04,251
Both at their shortest, it
shrinks by zero point two.

25
00:01:04,601 --> 00:01:08,122
So it is thirty five, plus
or minus zero point two.

26
00:01:08,122 --> 00:01:09,595
The errors added up.

27
00:01:10,145 --> 00:01:11,259
Now subtract.

28
00:01:11,259 --> 00:01:13,127
Lay rod B along rod A.

29
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:15,138
Five centimetres stick out.

30
00:01:15,487 --> 00:01:19,041
Longest case: A at its
longest, B at its shortest.

31
00:01:19,041 --> 00:01:20,222
Five point two.

32
00:01:20,572 --> 00:01:22,791
Shortest case: the other way round.

33
00:01:22,791 --> 00:01:23,945
Four point eight.

34
00:01:24,295 --> 00:01:27,253
So it is five, plus or
minus zero point two.

35
00:01:27,253 --> 00:01:29,760
Even when we subtract, the errors add.

36
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:33,134
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

37
00:01:33,484 --> 00:01:34,384
Step one.

38
00:01:34,364 --> 00:01:38,245
Z equals A plus B, with
errors delta A and delta B.

39
00:01:38,895 --> 00:01:39,795
Step two.

40
00:01:39,772 --> 00:01:43,815
The biggest Z is A plus
delta A, plus B plus delta B.

41
00:01:44,465 --> 00:01:45,434
Step three.

42
00:01:45,434 --> 00:01:48,605
So the error in Z is delta A plus delta B.

43
00:01:48,605 --> 00:01:50,838
The same holds for A minus B.

44
00:01:51,488 --> 00:01:52,549
Remember this.

45
00:01:52,549 --> 00:01:55,910
When you add or subtract,
the absolute errors add.

46
00:01:56,610 --> 00:01:57,758
Side by side.

47
00:01:57,758 --> 00:02:01,636
Adding gave thirty five,
plus or minus zero point two.

48
00:02:01,986 --> 00:02:03,462
Subtracting gave five.

49
00:02:03,462 --> 00:02:06,241
The same error, on a much smaller answer.

50
00:02:06,841 --> 00:02:07,958
Now you try.

51
00:02:07,958 --> 00:02:12,092
Fifty plus or minus one, minus
thirty plus or minus one.

52
00:02:12,092 --> 00:02:14,273
Is the error zero, or two?

53
00:02:14,273 --> 00:02:15,662
Pause and decide.

54
00:02:18,862 --> 00:02:19,762
Two!

55
00:02:19,363 --> 00:02:20,768
Errors never cancel.

56
00:02:20,768 --> 00:02:23,912
One reading can be high
while the other is low.

57
00:02:24,462 --> 00:02:25,614
Now multiply.

58
00:02:25,614 --> 00:02:27,352
Back to Aarav's table.

59
00:02:27,702 --> 00:02:32,197
One twenty by sixty is seven thousand
two hundred square centimetres.

60
00:02:32,547 --> 00:02:35,205
Now stretch both sides by one millimetre.

61
00:02:35,205 --> 00:02:36,808
Two thin strips appear.

62
00:02:37,158 --> 00:02:41,121
The long strip is one hundred
and twenty times zero point one.

63
00:02:41,121 --> 00:02:42,805
Twelve square centimetres.

64
00:02:43,155 --> 00:02:46,380
The short strip is sixty
times zero point one.

65
00:02:46,380 --> 00:02:47,280
Six.

66
00:02:46,912 --> 00:02:49,657
The tiny corner is too small to count.

67
00:02:50,007 --> 00:02:55,073
So the area can be off by eighteen square
centimetres, not one square millimetre!

68
00:02:55,623 --> 00:02:59,061
Make the table narrower, and
the percentage error climbs.

69
00:02:59,061 --> 00:03:01,047
A short side brings more error.

70
00:03:01,597 --> 00:03:04,531
Now the rule for
multiplying, step by step.

71
00:03:04,881 --> 00:03:05,823
Step one.

72
00:03:05,823 --> 00:03:07,545
Z equals A times B.

73
00:03:07,545 --> 00:03:11,231
The extra area is A
delta B, plus B delta A.

74
00:03:11,881 --> 00:03:12,792
Step two.

75
00:03:12,792 --> 00:03:16,234
Divide both sides by
Z, which is A times B.

76
00:03:16,884 --> 00:03:17,965
Step three.

77
00:03:17,965 --> 00:03:22,769
Delta Z over Z equals delta A
over A, plus delta B over B.

78
00:03:22,769 --> 00:03:24,754
The relative errors add.

79
00:03:25,404 --> 00:03:26,304
Step four.

80
00:03:26,285 --> 00:03:29,533
For Z equals A over B,
the rule is the same.

81
00:03:30,183 --> 00:03:31,298
Side by side.

82
00:03:31,298 --> 00:03:35,573
Adding or subtracting: add the
absolute errors, in centimetres.

83
00:03:35,923 --> 00:03:40,730
Multiplying or dividing: add the
relative errors, which have no unit.

84
00:03:40,730 --> 00:03:42,184
Or the percentages.

85
00:03:42,784 --> 00:03:43,684
Your turn.

86
00:03:43,652 --> 00:03:45,596
Speed is distance over time.

87
00:03:45,596 --> 00:03:48,668
Distance has one percent
error, time has two.

88
00:03:48,668 --> 00:03:51,540
Is the speed error one percent, or three?

89
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:52,440
Pause.

90
00:03:55,348 --> 00:03:56,475
Three percent!

91
00:03:56,475 --> 00:03:59,223
Division still adds the relative errors.

92
00:03:59,223 --> 00:04:00,729
They never subtract.

93
00:04:01,279 --> 00:04:03,066
Now, one worked example.

94
00:04:03,066 --> 00:04:04,903
Aarav's table, done fully.

95
00:04:05,253 --> 00:04:10,663
Length one twenty, width sixty, each
plus or minus zero point one centimetres.

96
00:04:11,113 --> 00:04:15,640
Relative error in length: zero point
one over one hundred and twenty.

97
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:19,955
Relative error in width:
zero point one over sixty.

98
00:04:20,605 --> 00:04:21,538
Add them.

99
00:04:21,538 --> 00:04:26,282
Zero point zero zero two five,
or zero point two five percent.

100
00:04:26,932 --> 00:04:32,346
Seven thousand two hundred times zero
point zero zero two five gives eighteen.

101
00:04:32,996 --> 00:04:37,541
So the area is seven thousand two
hundred, plus or minus eighteen square

102
00:04:37,541 --> 00:04:38,454
centimetres.

103
00:04:39,004 --> 00:04:40,469
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:40,819 --> 00:04:44,542
Many students subtract the
errors when they subtract values.

105
00:04:44,892 --> 00:04:46,656
But errors always add.

106
00:04:46,656 --> 00:04:49,640
Delta Z is still delta A plus delta B.

107
00:04:50,190 --> 00:04:51,710
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:51,710 --> 00:04:55,533
As numericals on the maximum
percentage error in a result.

109
00:04:56,133 --> 00:04:57,372
Exam level now.

110
00:04:57,372 --> 00:05:01,623
Find the maximum percentage error
in the trolley's average speed.

111
00:05:01,623 --> 00:05:02,914
Pause and solve.

112
00:05:08,314 --> 00:05:09,648
The answer is C.

113
00:05:09,648 --> 00:05:11,344
One point four percent.

114
00:05:11,794 --> 00:05:14,211
The distance is fifty centimetres.

115
00:05:14,211 --> 00:05:18,862
Its error is zero point one plus
zero point one, zero point two.

116
00:05:19,312 --> 00:05:21,542
That is zero point four percent.

117
00:05:21,542 --> 00:05:25,650
The time error is zero point
zero two over two, one percent.

118
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:28,422
Speed is a division, so add them.

119
00:05:28,422 --> 00:05:29,995
One point four percent.

120
00:05:30,595 --> 00:05:32,055
Here is your recap.

121
00:05:32,055 --> 00:05:33,556
Screenshot this card.

122
00:05:36,756 --> 00:05:39,754
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

123
00:05:40,104 --> 00:05:41,004
One.

124
00:05:40,596 --> 00:05:43,422
Why do errors add, even when you subtract?

125
00:05:44,322 --> 00:05:45,222
Two.

126
00:05:44,825 --> 00:05:47,556
When do you add percentage errors instead?

127
00:05:48,456 --> 00:05:49,356
Three.

128
00:05:49,068 --> 00:05:52,483
What is the error in the
perimeter of Aarav's table?

129
00:05:53,583 --> 00:05:56,712
Next time: your calculator
shows eight digits.

130
00:05:56,712 --> 00:05:58,444
Which ones do you keep?

131
00:05:58,444 --> 00:06:00,044
Measurement uncertainty.
